WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:02.540
Marcus has another question.

2
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.960
If he pays much less in premiums than the death benefit,

3
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.940
how can his family receive a larger covered payment?

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.280
The answer starts with risk pooling.

5
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.820
An insurer combines many exposures and spreads the financial burden of

6
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.040
covered losses across the group.

7
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.300
Marcus's own premiums are not the limit of a covered claim.

8
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.520
The insurer owes the benefit according to the policy contract.

9
00:00:33.282 --> 00:00:38.402
Think of many individual policies supporting one insurance operation.

10
00:00:39.062 --> 00:00:44.002
Premiums help fund its obligations, alongside other financial resources.

11
00:00:44.962 --> 00:00:48.722
The policy is not simply a personal savings account that pays

12
00:00:48.722 --> 00:00:51.342
back only what Marcus put in.

13
00:00:51.982 --> 00:00:57.462
Pooling does not prevent death, require identical premiums,

14
00:00:57.462 --> 00:01:02.682
or erase uncertainty. People can have different coverage

15
00:01:02.682 --> 00:01:05.202
amounts and risk classifications.

16
00:01:06.082 --> 00:01:10.662
The insurer must still price its policies and manage its resources

17
00:01:10.662 --> 00:01:14.632
carefully. Now connect pooling to the law of large numbers.

18
00:01:15.312 --> 00:01:20.492
A larger group of comparable, largely independent exposures tends to have

19
00:01:20.492 --> 00:01:26.832
more predictable average loss experience.
The key word is average.

20
00:01:27.932 --> 00:01:30.852
A large pool may have more total claims than a small

21
00:01:30.852 --> 00:01:35.792
pool. The useful improvement is greater predictability in relative terms.

22
00:01:36.452 --> 00:01:39.332
It does not tell the insurer who will die, or guarantee

23
00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:40.972
an exact number of claims.

24
00:01:41.958 --> 00:01:43.398
Use an invented example.

25
00:01:44.118 --> 00:01:47.758
Suppose one thousand people each have a one percent annual probability

26
00:01:47.758 --> 00:01:48.918
of a death claim.

27
00:01:49.918 --> 00:01:53.198
One thousand times one percent gives ten expected claims.

28
00:01:54.038 --> 00:01:57.118
Expected means an average across many possible outcomes.

29
00:01:58.078 --> 00:02:02.038
This year's count could be eight, ten, twelve, or another number.

30
00:02:02.898 --> 00:02:06.698
These are teaching assumptions, not real mortality figures or a premium

31
00:02:06.698 --> 00:02:09.738
quote. No individual outcome is identified.

32
00:02:10.686 --> 00:02:13.404
Compare one extra claim in two groups

33
00:02:13.404 --> 00:02:16.800
with the same assumed one percent probability.

34
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.676
In a group of one hundred, moving from

35
00:02:19.676 --> 00:02:22.896
one claim to two changes the rate from one percent to

36
00:02:22.896 --> 00:02:26.556
two percent. In a group of ten thousand, moving from one

37
00:02:26.556 --> 00:02:29.792
hundred claims to one hundred one changes the rate to one

38
00:02:29.792 --> 00:02:31.136
point zero one percent.

39
00:02:31.136 --> 00:02:34.456
One extra claim has a smaller effect on the larger group's

40
00:02:34.456 --> 00:02:39.500
rate. This illustrates sensitivity, not a guarantee.

41
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.356
Shared events can still cause many claims together.

42
00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:44.890
The makeup of the group matters too.

43
00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.450
Imagine an insurance offer at a standard price.

44
00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.850
People who know they have a greater chance of loss are

45
00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.230
especially eager to apply.

46
00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.170
That tendency is adverse selection.

47
00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.230
It can occur even when everyone answers truthfully.

48
00:02:56.470 --> 00:03:00.730
Underwriting helps the insurer evaluate applications and classify risk using

49
00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.390
permitted information and rules.

50
00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:04.330
It does not eliminate uncertainty.

51
00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:08.990
Remember the distinctions: pooling spreads financial risk; large numbers

52
00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:13.430
improve group estimates; adverse selection concerns who seeks coverage.

53
00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:17.370
Now answer the eight lesson questions,
and review any mistakes before finishing.
